WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.569
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:11.089
This is a fully in-house Disney paper on how
to teach a neural network to capture the appearance

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:12.309
of clouds.

00:00:12.309 --> 00:00:17.230
This topic is one of my absolute favorites
because it is in the intersection of the two

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:21.480
topics I love most - computer graphics and
machine learning.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.619
Hell yeah!

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:27.200
Generally, we use light simulation programs
to render these clouds, and the difficult

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.960
part of this is that we have to perform something
that is called volumetric path tracing.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.829
This is a technique where we have to simulate
rays of light that do not necessarily bounce

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:43.860
off of the surface of objects, but may penetrate
their surfaces and undergo many scattering

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:44.860
events.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:50.129
Understandably, in the case of clouds, capturing
volumetric scattering properly is a key element

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:52.050
in modeling their physical appearance.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:57.440
However, we have to simulate millions and
millions of light paths with potentially hundreds

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.520
of scattering events, which is a computationally
demanding task even in the age of rapidly

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.910
improving hardware.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:08.660
As you can see here, the more we bump up the
number of possible simulated scattering events,

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.380
the closer we get to reality, but the longer
it takes to render an image.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.470
In the case of bright clouds here, rendering
an image like this can take up to 30 hours.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:24.650
In this work, a nice hybrid approach is proposed
where a neural network learns the concept

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:30.150
of in-scattered radiance and predicts it rapidly
so this part we don't have to compute ourselves.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.810
It is a hybrid because some parts of the renderer
are still using the traditional algorithms.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:41.610
The dataset used for training the neural network
contains 75 different clouds, some of which

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:47.220
are procedurally generated by a computer,
and some are drawn by artists to expose the

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.020
learning algorithm to a large variety of cases.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.060
As a result, these images can be rendered
in a matter of seconds to minutes.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.880
Normally, this would take many-many hours
on a powerful computer.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.080
Here's another result with traditional path
tracing.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.000
And now the same with deep scattering.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.220
Yep, that's how long it takes.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.520
The scattering parameters can also be interactively
edited without us having to wait for hours

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.410
to see if the new settings are better than
the previous ones.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:29.580
Dialing in the perfect results typically takes
an extremely lengthy trial and error phase

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.060
which now can be done almost instantaneously.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.960
The technique also supports a variety of different
scattering models.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.360
As with all results, they have to be compared
to the ground truth renderings, and as you

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.620
can see here, they seem mostly indistinguishable
from reality.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:54.240
It is also temporally stable, so animation
rendering can take place flicker-free as is

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.920
demonstrated here in the video.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:01.640
I think this work is also a great testament
to show how these incredible learning algorithms

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.790
can accelerate progress in practically all
fields of science.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.800
And given that this work was done by Disney,
I am pretty sure we can expect tons of photorealistic

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.620
clouds in their upcoming movies in the near
future.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.830
There are tons of more details discussed in
the paper, which is remarkably well produced,

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.710
make sure to have a look, the link is in the
video description.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.830
This is a proper, proper paper, you don't
want to miss out on this one.

